421,-1.1920928244535389e-07,-1.5497195136049413e-06,0.0,-0.006363131105899811,-0.02825426124036312,-0.010767456144094467,-1.1920928244535389e-07,-1.1920928244535389e-07,-2.0265558760002023e-06,-1.728519782773219e-05,-0.005136745050549507,-1.7881377516459906e-06,-1.1920928244535389e-07,-7.152555099310121e-07,-1.1920928244535389e-07,0.0,-7.152555099310121e-07,-1.7881377516459906e-06,-3.6954811548639555e-06,-2.3841855067985307e-07,0.0,0.0,-0.00012635385792236775,-1.4305104514278355e-06,-2.3841855067985307e-07,0.0,-1.7881377516459906e-06,-3.576278118089249e-07,-2.95634672511369e-05,-0.3308463394641876,-1.4305104514278355e-06,-0.000427274004323408,-1.8079643249511719,-0.003462512046098709,-0.0005600076401606202,-2.104950189590454,-0.01853678748011589,0.0,0.0,-8.821448318485636e-06,-2.50339189733495e-06,0.0,-0.00038580605178140104,-1.537788011773955e-05,-0.00013934595335740596],"request":{"active_prefix":"","input_ids":[151644,8948,198,2610,525,1207,16948,11,3465,553,54364,14817,13,1446,525,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,641,1246,1657,5510,646,400,22,3,1251,2444,2163,264,4778,1965,421,902,1378,315,279,400,18,3,1251,36977,11,46830,11,323,11108,646,2444,1790,311,1817,1008,30,320,1514,1095,27804,892,525,69201,315,1817,1008,525,11758,438,279,1852,6138,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nIn how many ways can $7$ people sit around a round table if no two of the $3$ people Pierre, Rosa, and Thomas can sit next to each other? (Seating arrangements which are rotations of each other are treated as the same.)","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"6ba371631a2e79ca9f7a3480d26155f9f979bf41eae65c0437cabae773d79fc8"},"seconds":32.07599687576294,"seed":171566087491685885,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve this problem, we'll first calculate the total number of seating arrangements of $7$ people around a round table, and then subtract the number of arrangements where at least two of Pierre, Rosa, and Thomas (P, R, T) are seated next to each other.\n\n### Step 1: Total number of arrangements\nFor $7$ people sitting around a round table, the number of distinct arrangements is given by $(7-1)! = 6! = 720$.\n\n### Step 2: Arrangements where at least two of P, R, T are next to each other\nWe will use the principle of inclusion-exclusion to count these arrangements.\n\n#### Case 1: Two of P, R, T are next to each other\nTreat the block of two (say P and R) as a single entity. This gives us $6$ \"people\" to arrange around the table, which can be done in $(6-1)! = 5! = 120$ ways. There are $\\binom{3}{2} = 3$ ways to choose which two of P, R, T are next to each other, and $2$ ways to arrange P and R within their block. Thus, the number of arrangements for this case is:\n\\[ 3 \\times 2 \\times 120 = 720. \\]\n\n#### Case 2: All three of P, R, T are next to each other\nTreat the block of three (P, R, T) as a single entity. This gives us $5$ \"people\" to arrange around the table, which can be done in $(5-1)! = 4! = 24$ ways. There are $3! = 6$ ways to arrange P, R, and T within their block. Thus, the number of arrangements for this case is:\n\\[ 24 \\times 6 = 144. \\]\n\nUsing inclusion-exclusion, the number of arrangements where at least two of P, R, T are next to each other is:\n\\[ 720 - 144 = 576. \\]\n\n### Step 3: Subtract from the total\nThe number of valid arrangements where no two of P, R, T are next to each other is:\n\\[ 720 - 576 = 144. \\]\n\nThus, the final answer is \\(\\boxed{144}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{144}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
